    "user_request_original": "Design a 21-layer Ta2O5/SiO2 defect-cavity bandpass filter on fused silica substrate for 1550 nm LiDAR receiver. Stack sequence: Air | Ta2O5 | SiO2 | Ta2O5 | SiO2 | Ta2O5 | SiO2 | Ta2O5 | SiO2 | Ta2O5 | SiO2 | Ta2O5 (defect cavity) | SiO2 | Ta2O5 | SiO2 | Ta2O5 | SiO2 | Ta2O5 | SiO2 | Ta2O5 | SiO2 | Ta2O5 | Fused Silica. Initialize all high-index layers to 185 nm and low-index layers to 267 nm (quarter-wave at 1550 nm), except defect cavity at 534 nm (half-wave). Optimize all 21 layer thicknesses with bounds 50-400 nm for DBR layers and 400-650 nm for defect cavity. Enforce adjacent-layer thickness difference constraint: |d[i+1] - d[i]| <= 40 nm for layers 5 through 16. Evaluate at normal incidence, unpolarized. Target: maximize mean transmittance in 1550+/-5 nm band, minimize mean transmittance in 1400-1500 nm and 1600-1700 nm bands. Canonical user controls: wavelength intervals 1400-1500 nm, 1550-1550 nm, 1600-1700 nm. soft objectives: high transmittance at 1550 nm (soft target ≥ 0.95); strong suppression of transmittance in 1400-1500 nm and 1600-1700 nm. behaviors to suppress or avoid: transmittance in 1400-1500 nm; transmittance in 1600-1700 nm. manufacturing constraints: total layers ≤ 30.",
